For optimistic updates, you separate the optimistic UI state from the actual server state. Here's a useReducer implementation:

```javascript
const initialState = {
  data: null,
  status: 'idle', // 'idle' | 'pending' | 'success' | 'error'
  previousData: null,
  error: null
};

function reducer(state, action) {
  switch (action.type) {
    case 'OPTIMISTIC_UPDATE':
      return {
        ...state,
        previousData: state.data,
        data: action.payload,
        status: 'pending'
      };
    case 'SUCCESS':
      return {
        ...state,
        status: 'success',
        previousData: null
      };
    case 'ERROR':
      return {
        ...state,
        status: 'error',
        data: state.previousData, // rollback
        previousData: null,
        error: action.error
      };
    default:
      return state;
  }
}

// Usage in component:
const [state, dispatch] = useReducer(reducer, initialState);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  dispatch({ type: 'OPTIMISTIC_UPDATE', payload: newData });
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
    dispatch({ type: 'SUCCESS' });
  } catch (error) {
    dispatch({ type: 'ERROR', error });
  }
};
```

The useState approach uses useEffect for the server call:

```javascript
const [data, setData] = useState(null);
const [isPending, setIsPending] = useState(false);
const [error, setError] = useState(null);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  const previousData = data;
  setData(newData); // optimistic update
  setIsPending(true);
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
  } catch (error) {
    setData(previousData); // rollback
    setError(error);
  } finally {
    setIsPending(false);
  }
};
```

For race conditions with multiple rapid updates, use AbortController to cancel in-flight requests:

```javascript
const abortRef = useRef(null);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  // Cancel any in-flight request
  abortRef.current?.abort();

  const controller = new AbortController();
  abortRef.current = controller;

  setData(newData); // optimistic
  try {
    await api.update(newData, { signal: controller.signal });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error.name !== 'AbortError') {
      setData(previousData);
    }
  }
};
```

Alternatively, use version/sequence tracking to ignore stale responses:

```javascript
const versionRef = useRef(0);

const handleUpdate = async (newData) => {
  const currentVersion = ++versionRef.current;
  setData(newData);
  try {
    await api.update(newData);
    if (currentVersion !== versionRef.current) return; // stale response
    // process success
  } catch (error) {
    if (currentVersion !== versionRef.current) return;
    setData(previousData); // rollback
  }
};
```

Key strategies: 1) Store previous state before optimistic update, 2) Use AbortController to cancel stale requests, 3) Track request version to ignore outdated responses, 4) Consider a queue for sequential processing of dependent updates.

## Concept Overview
A custom useDebounce hook defers function execution until a specified delay period has elapsed, making it essential for optimizing search inputs, API calls, and other performance-critical operations in React applications.

## Implementation Details
- Utilize useRef to maintain the latest callback reference, preventing stale closures across renders
- Implement comprehensive cleanup logic in useEffect to cancel pending debounced invocations
- Return a useCallback wrapper with stable dependencies for optimal concurrent rendering performance
- Handle component unmounting scenarios to prevent memory leaks and unexpected behavior
- Ensure proper cleanup of timers to maintain application stability

## Code Example
```javascript
import { useCallback, useRef, useEffect } from 'react';

export function useDebounce(callback, delay) {
  const callbackRef = useRef(callback);
  const timeoutRef = useRef(null);

  useEffect(() => {
    callbackRef.current = callback;
  }, [callback]);

  useEffect(() => {
    return () => {
      if (timeoutRef.current) {
        clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
      }
    };
  }, []);

  return useCallback((...args) => {
    if (timeoutRef.current) {
      clearTimeout(timeoutRef.current);
    }

    timeoutRef.current = setTimeout(() => {
      callbackRef.current(...args);
    }, delay);
  }, [delay]);
}
```

## Key Benefits
- Prevents stale closures through proper ref management
- Ensures cleanup on component unmount
- Maintains stable reference for concurrent rendering
- Handles edge cases and prevents memory leaks

